6420494 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420494 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420494 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186420481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420494 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e0f59fc3-d642-5f6b-9b06-fc7d67132d2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420495 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420495 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420495 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420495 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420495 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343408" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420495 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420495 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $F(x)={x}^{2}$ defined on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ).$\nThe Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\cdot 0)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(0)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}}={{field-4}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420495 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "17f63cde-bca7-5760-8481-ec7b747c5fc4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420495 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\cdot 0)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(0)=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\cdot 0) \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot 1 \\\\ -\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3} & =4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}} & =-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{12} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}} & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420496 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420496 #edb/ref 1069 "q-226151" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420496 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420496 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/de/de3e018fa1382ee2250d1b2e256e3b47cafef346852007f0e6afb84342d7cb04.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=3x\\,i-2y\\,j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $4$ and height $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420496 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6071adb1-29d3-5cda-a599-699b41d64b95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420496 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/09/0950c559d5d6df93cf40afd8fdbd07f090f864fa462083bfd496246e7f2bded9.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(3x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=36.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(3x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+3j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-24.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 36+2\\cdot (-24)=24.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420497 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186420505 #edb/ref 175921860454